WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:06.990
Democrats who were looking forward to the

00:00:06.990 --> 00:00:08.610
election of Hillary Clinton and

00:00:08.610 --> 00:00:11.010
expecting that it would happen, have some

00:00:11.010 --> 00:00:13.559
obstacles ahead of them in 2018 for

00:00:13.559 --> 00:00:16.170
maintaining majorities in Congress. Now

00:00:16.170 --> 00:00:18.000
with the election of Donald Trump, it

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:19.470
looks a little more forbidding for them.

00:00:19.470 --> 00:00:22.230
The Senate seats that are up in 2018,

00:00:22.230 --> 00:00:23.970
you've got ten Democratic senators

00:00:23.970 --> 00:00:25.680
running for re-election in states that

00:00:25.680 --> 00:00:28.320
were carried by Donald Trump. That's not

00:00:28.320 --> 00:00:30.150
a good place to start off for the

00:00:30.150 --> 00:00:31.679
Democratic Party. You have very few

00:00:31.679 --> 00:00:33.510
Republican seats at risk.

00:00:33.510 --> 00:00:35.280
It's the opposite of the situation this

00:00:35.280 --> 00:00:37.469
year. And so the likelihood is that the

00:00:37.469 --> 00:00:39.840
Republicans will gain seats. If the Trump

00:00:39.840 --> 00:00:42.000
administration is seen as successful

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:43.739
and producing good results,

00:00:43.739 --> 00:00:45.660
the Democrats might lose quite a few seats.

00:00:45.660 --> 00:00:47.760
If the Trump administration is seen as

00:00:47.760 --> 00:00:50.370
unsuccessful, Democrats might hold losses

00:00:50.370 --> 00:00:52.739
down towards zero. So, we'll see about

00:00:52.739 --> 00:00:53.820
that. In the House of Representatives:

00:00:53.820 --> 00:00:56.760
remember, Mitt Romney carried a majority

00:00:56.760 --> 00:00:59.340
of House seats—226. Donald Trump carried

00:00:59.340 --> 00:01:01.350
more than that. That's going to make it

00:01:01.350 --> 00:01:03.090
tough for the Democrats to gain the

00:01:03.090 --> 00:01:07.000
seats they need for a majority in the house.

